trace.content:
[THOUGHT] Absence of new exposures and lack of atopic history makes classic allergic contact dermatitis or atopic dermatitis less likely—though not impossible (e.g., delayed sensitization or low-grade chronic irritant exposure). Spongiotic histology remains consistent, but we must consider other causes of chronic symmetric spongiotic dermatitis:  
- Scabies (can present chronically with pruritic, symmetrical upper arm involvement; burrows may be subtle or absent in crusted or nodular variants);  
- Fungal infection (tinea corporis can mimic eczema clinically and sometimes show spongiosis secondarily — “id reaction” or misdiagnosed tinea);  
- Lichen simplex chronicus (but usually shows hyperkeratosis and acanthosis, not prominent spongiosis);  
- Early mycosis fungoides (rare, but can present as chronic pruritic patches; histology may show atypical lymphocytes — not yet evident here).  

Given the bilateral upper arm location and chronic itch, scabies remains a key consideration — especially if household contacts are asymptomatic or if infestation is low-burden. Also, tinea should be ruled out clinically and mycologically.

[TEST] Potassium hydroxide (KOH) preparation of skin scrapings from an active lesion on the right upper arm.

